# Background Remover deliberately does NOT send Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. Those two headers are what a document needs
# before the browser hands it SharedArrayBuffer, and without SharedArrayBuffer
# ONNX Runtime's WASM build falls back to a single thread no matter how many
# cores the visitor has — so isolating this route is the obvious way to make
# the model faster.
#
# It was tried, and it broke the tool. Once isolation was genuinely in effect
# (an earlier attempt sent each header twice, which browsers reject, so the
# page was never isolated and the slow single-threaded path kept working), the
# runtime took its multi-threaded path and background removal started failing
# outright on real hardware. That failure has still never been reproduced: the
# threaded path has since been exercised end to end on a four-core Windows
# Chrome machine with these headers served locally, and it works — correct
# cutout, and inference roughly twice as fast as one thread. The machine that
# broke has sixteen cores, and nothing short of that machine has reproduced it.
#
# Two things did come out of that run and are now fixed in the code, so a
# second attempt is not the same attempt:
#   - Thread count. The library asks ONNX Runtime for one thread per logical
#     core and exposes no way to override it. Measured on four cores, one image:
#     17.9s at one thread, 8.8s at two, 11.6s at four, 25.9s at sixteen. So a
#     sixteen-core visitor was asking for the slowest setting available —
#     slower than not threading at all. `plannedThreadCount` now caps it.
#   - No safety net. A threaded attempt that fails for any reason other than the
#     user canceling is now retried on a single thread automatically, so the
#     worst case is the speed the tool has today rather than a broken tool.
#
# What that does NOT cover is a failure that kills the renderer outright (an
# out-of-memory tab crash runs no JavaScript, so nothing retries). That is why
# these stay off until someone runs the tool from upload to output on the
# sixteen-core machine that broke. `PROJECT_STATUS.md` carries the procedure.
#
# If they are restored: exactly ONE rule may match the page. `/tools/background-remover/*`
# already covers the trailing-slash URL; adding `/tools/background-remover/` as
# well makes both rules match, each policy is sent twice, and the joined value
# `same-origin, same-origin` is invalid — the browser then falls back to
# unsafe-none and the page is not isolated at all.
